A||||12||docprego||Stereo Help||||1||
Z||000000||docprego||04-01-2001||02:47 AM||||Hello,<P>I am looking for advice on what to do with the audio system in my 88 GT.<P>I have the factory sub and premium AM/FM cassette with eq.<P>It doesn't sound very good.  What can I do to improve it dramatically.<P>What about the head unit, is there a good drop in replacement.  Will those large GM sized units I see some car stereo manufacturers making, fit?  Should I keeep the factory sub and get Rodney's replacement speaker?  If I do this, how do I interface the sub system with the new deck.<P>BTW, I want to install a CD unit, thanks for any suggestions.<P>docprego<BR>Pics of my 88 GT: <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=112028&a=12364533" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=112028&a=12364533</A>  <A HREF="http://www.playitagain-games.com" TARGET=_blank>www.playitagain-games.com</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||jeffozz||04-01-2001||07:45 AM||||If you want to keep a stock look, this is what I did:<BR>1.  Replace all of the speakers.  Pioneer, Kenwood, etc all make nice replacement speakers that fit into the Fiero....  Also, replace the sub speaker with Rodney's.<BR>2.  Replace the radio itself with a newer model Delco CD w/7 band EQ found in Pontiac Grand Am's, Trans Am's, etc... up until 2000.  This unit is a significant upgrade over the original and looks like it belongs in there...  You will need to buy a wire harness adapter for the newer radio and make a small modification to the subwoofer harness to get the sub to funtion properly.<BR>All in all, this is a significant upgrade with resonable costs ($300-500?) depending how well you shop...||||reg||1
Z||000002||stimpy||04-01-2001||10:26 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by docprego:<BR><B><BR>What about the head unit, is there a good drop in replacement.  Will those large GM sized units I see some car stereo manufacturers making, fit? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I recently had the good fortune to get a Pioneer DEH-P77-DH.  Perfect fit and incredible sound. Lots of adjustments to play with too. $230 shipped from <A HREF="http://www.competitionsound.com" TARGET=_blank>www.competitionsound.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||docprego||04-01-2001||01:41 PM||||Jeff:  Is this the exact unit you are referring to?<BR> <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=1225392232" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=1225392232</A> <P>Could you tell me in more detail about the modification to the subwoofer harness?  I am unfamiliar with how Pontiac interfaced the sub with the stereo.  Will the sub volume slider be operational in the new setup?<P>Stimpy:  Did you use the Pioneer's built in amp, 45x4 sounds like a good amount of power and I really like the look of the unit.  You are saying that it was a drop in fit?<P>Another question:  The sub volume slider for my subwoofer seems to have little to no effect on the sound coming from the stereo system.  Is this a good indication that it's time to order Rodney's sub replacement or could it be the slider itself?<P>Thanks,<BR>docprego <A HREF="http://www.playitagain-games.com" TARGET=_blank>www.playitagain-games.com</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by docprego (edited 04-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||04-01-2001||01:53 PM||||Get a new headunit, one that puts out some power. Like Pioneer's GM fit ones. They are awesome. Second, I would put an 8" sub in the stock location, and get a small aftermarket amp to power it, ditch the cheesy stock one. I am re-doing the system in my car, since it is just sitting. I used to have 6-1/2" components in the kickpanels, and 4" Polk EX componets in the dash and rear speaker locations. I am going to keep them in the rear, but Im putting in MB Quart 3way components up front. 6-1/2" woofers(kickpanels), 4" midranges(dash), and 1" tweeters also in the dash. Unless you are really looking for an awesome sounding system, 3way components might be a little overkill. I would definatly use 4" compoents, or just 4" round 2/3ways in the dash, instead of 4x10,6x9,4x6, things like that. Round speakers will give better acoustical performance. You should let your sub handle about 15Hz-80Hz frequencys. Midbass from about 80Hz-400Hz, Midrange from 400Hz-3000Hz. Tweeters 3kHz-30kHz.<P>All and all, I would reccomend getting 2 sets of 4" components, for the front and back. 8" subwoofer in a ported enclosure under the dash. Small amp for subwoofer. New headunit with some power. This system could be acheived under $600 total. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||docprego||04-01-2001||02:06 PM||||Sean:<P>When replacing the sub with an 8" in the ported enclosure, does this involve ditching the factory enclosure?<P>docprego <A HREF="http://www.playitagain-games.com" TARGET=_blank>www.playitagain-games.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||04-01-2001||02:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by docprego:<BR><B>Stimpy:  Did you use the Pioneer's built in amp, 45x4 sounds like a good amount of power and I really like the look of the unit.  You are saying that it was a drop in fit?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Perfect drop-in fit. The 45w per channel is acceptable, but I do have amps running the Pioneer 4x10's, the 10" sub, and the Aura Bass Shakers. If you do not think you would need the 3 RCA outputs, the DEH-P47 DH is about $80 less and has most of the feature. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||04-01-2001||03:49 PM||||Yes, It requires building a custom one. I played with it for a while, and it sounds descent. I dont use it in my car becuase my system runs dual 12's for SPL only, and a bandpass 10" for everyday listening. You can build the enclosure out of PVC pipe, 8" in dia. You should try and tune it to the sub, but with the very limited space available up there, theres not much you can do. I am still messing with the idea on paper to make it sound better, but I don't get in a very big hurry unless I'm rushed. (And my rescent house fire put a <I>small</I> dent into my personal stereo work, but things are getting back on track. If you are not very inclined into building your own enclosures, I could do it for ya. Soonest I could start would be about 1 to 1-1/2 weeks from now. It doesnt take very long to build, but a heck of a long time to design and plan out. You could also use stimpy's method of a footwell installation. It looks almost completely stock, with the exception of the big speaker there. He has by far the cleanest footwell installation in a Fiero Iv seen. His is sealed, but I think there is enough volume down there, without taking up much legroom, to use a ported box. I would take ported over sealed any day of the week, but it all depends on what kinda music you listen to.||||reg||1
Z||000008||docprego||04-01-2001||03:54 PM||||Cool, I think I am more inclined to preserve the factory sub enclosure.  With this in mind, and knowing I sacrifice sound quality, what is the optimal thing I can do to the factory sub to improve it.  Is there something better than Rodney's replacement speaker?<P>I am leaning towards the DEH-P77 cuz of the remote, it is only about $30 more than the 47 at competition sound.com<P>docprego <A HREF="http://www.playitagain-games.com" TARGET=_blank>www.playitagain-games.com</A> <p>[This message has been edited by docprego (edited 04-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Seanh||04-01-2001||04:04 PM||||Rodney's sub is very nice for the money. But<BR>JL or JBL, I can't keep those 2 straight! One of them makes a very nice sub that would fit the factory enclosure, but its quite a bit more expensive then Rodney's. His does sound alot better then the stock one, and for $36 including all the installation equipment, you can't beat it with anything. <BR>Also get a larger amp if you get Rodney's or the JBL/JL (whoever it is) becuase they will both handle alot more power then the stock one will. A descent amp might run you $80 for it.<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 04-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||GodSend||04-02-2001||12:23 AM||||Stimpy,<P>How are you liking those Bass Shakers?  Did you get them from partsexpress (the larger ones $29 or the smaller ones?)<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.hypernetcommunications.com/revn.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||jeffozz||04-02-2001||01:03 AM||||Yes, that is the Delco I was referring to...  The modification to the sub harness is very easy...  You have to splice two wires (pink) into one (yellow). Here is some great info on the sub system in the Fiero: <A HREF="http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/sub.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/sub.html</A> <BR>After the installation, the slider will funtion normally.  The slider only controls the volune to the sub itself.<BR>Hope this helps and good luck!<BR>Jeff;o)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||stimpy||04-02-2001||09:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GodSend:<BR><B>Stimpy,<P>How are you liking those Bass Shakers?  Did you get them from partsexpress (the larger ones $29 or the smaller ones?)<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have a set of the 50w "Pro Series" that comes with a matched amp. They work very well in conjunction with a sub. Funny thing is, now my wife likes rap. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
